            ProjectUri   = 'https://github.com/Fortigi/PSComplexity'
            ReleaseNotes = '0.7.0: **`-Detailed` reports each unit''s lines of code.** Complexity says nothing about size -- a long function of straight-line code scores 1 and 0 -- so `-Detailed` now adds `LinesOfCode`, counted exactly as SonarQube counts `ncloc`: a physical line holding at least one character that is neither whitespace nor part of a comment. A brace on its own line, each line of a continued statement and the non-blank lines of a here-string count; blank lines, comments and comment-based help do not. Each line belongs to the innermost unit, so a nested function''s lines are not its parent''s. It is information only, with no threshold, and a run without `-Detailed` is unchanged. The JSON report carries it for a `-Detailed` run, as an optional field of each unit. **The gate''s policy can live in a committed `.pscomplexity.json`.** Ceilings, per-path overrides, excludes, acceptances with their reasons and the baseline file, in one place for CI and for a run by hand. `Test-PSComplexity` takes `-ConfigFile`, or finds the nearest `.pscomplexity.json` from each path up to the repository root -- never above it, and not at all outside a repository -- and `-NoConfig` turns that off. The file used is printed and recorded in the JSON report as `config`, beside `scope.exclude`, `scope.excluded` and `thresholds.overrides`. The command line wins wherever it says something. A config that is not valid is refused, naming every fault, and its format ships as `schemas/v1/config.schema.json`. Without a config nothing changes: the ceilings are still 15 and 15. Measure-PSComplexity reads no config. **An acceptance can be written in the code.** A `# PSCx-Accept: <reason>` line comment directly above a function, method or constructor excuses that unit, with exactly the checks `-Accept` has: the gate fails when the unit is back within its ceilings or the reason is empty. The argument then moves with the code instead of going stale in a `File` + `Unit` list. A declaration that excuses nothing -- separated by a blank line, sharing a line with code, or above something that is not a unit -- is refused rather than ignored, and so is a unit accepted both in its code and in a list. Whole units only. The report''s `accepted` entries now say where each argument is written, as `source` (`inline` or `list`) and the `line` of an inline one. **A Markdown summary, for a CI step summary or a pull request comment.** `-MarkdownPath` on both commands writes the run as Markdown: the verdict and every unit over a ceiling (the gate), the ten most complex units (`-MarkdownTop` changes the number), per-file totals, and what the numbers do not cover. With `-CompareTo` and a previous JSON report it also lists the units that are new, worse or better than in that report; a report from another metric version is refused. Rendered from the same scan as the records and the JSON report, so it holds no number they do not. **Gate only the functions a change touched.** `-ChangedDiff` on both commands takes a unified diff -- `git diff --unified=0 origin/main...HEAD` -- and restricts the run to the units owning a changed line, where `-ChangedFile` takes every unit in a touched file. A changed line belongs to the innermost unit holding it, so editing a nested function does not drag in its parent; a pure deletion belongs to the unit it was deleted from; a line outside every function belongs to the script body. Only the `+++` and `@@` headers are read, and the module still runs no git. An empty diff, text that is not a diff, a PowerShell file the diff names that is not there, and `-ChangedFile` beside it are refused. Acceptances and baseline entries are still checked against every unit of the changed files. The JSON report records the hunks as `scope.changedLines`. Full changelog: https://github.com/Fortigi/PSComplexity/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md'
